WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.857
Five hundred and one months of American inflation,

00:02.957 --> 00:06.170
since nineteen eighty-five. They average two point seven

00:06.270 --> 00:09.186
eight percent. But how many independent facts about

00:09.286 --> 00:12.203
that average do five hundred months really contain?

00:13.300 --> 00:15.500
The answer is fewer than you think, because

00:15.600 --> 00:17.640
every month echoes the months before it.

00:18.733 --> 00:23.430
Here are forty-eight months, coloured from cold to hot. On top they

00:23.530 --> 00:28.513
are independent: hot and cold arrive in no particular order. Below, the

00:28.613 --> 00:33.096
same number of months with memory, rho equal to point eight. Now

00:33.196 --> 00:36.318
hot months come in runs, and so do cold ones.

00:36.400 --> 00:40.225
Each run is one surprise, echoed. The forty-eight months carry

00:40.325 --> 00:44.150
the information of only about five independent ones. The ratio

00:44.250 --> 00:47.062
is gamma zero over the long-run variance, V x.

00:48.133 --> 00:51.429
Where does V x come from? The variance of a sum is

00:51.529 --> 00:55.843
the sum of every entry in the covariance grid. The diagonal holds

00:55.943 --> 00:59.919
gamma zero, the next diagonals gamma one, and so on outward.

01:01.000 --> 01:05.565
Slide each diagonal out into a column. Diagonal j has T minus

01:05.665 --> 01:09.848
j cells, so the columns form a triangle. Divide by T and

01:09.948 --> 01:14.819
let T grow, and the triangle flattens: the variance of the scaled

01:14.919 --> 01:19.943
sum tends to the sum of all autocovariances, the long-run variance.

01:21.033 --> 01:25.957
For a first-order autoregression the echoes are rho to the j. Stack every

01:26.057 --> 01:29.468
one of them on top of gamma zero and the tower is V

01:29.568 --> 01:33.184
x: one plus rho, over one minus rho, times gamma zero.

01:33.266 --> 01:36.823
Raise rho and the tower soars. Make rho negative and the

01:36.923 --> 01:41.003
echoes alternate in sign and cancel: the tower drops below gamma

01:41.103 --> 01:45.052
zero, and the mean is known better than with independent data.

01:46.133 --> 01:49.633
In practice we estimate the echoes from the data. Near lags

01:49.733 --> 01:52.927
are well measured; far lags rest on a handful of pairs

01:53.027 --> 01:56.100
and are mostly noise. Now add them all up, the naive

01:56.200 --> 01:59.883
estimate. The running sum climbs, then slides all the way back

01:59.983 --> 02:03.483
to exactly zero. It always does: the residuals sum to zero.

02:04.566 --> 02:08.872
Newey and West tame the noise with a triangle. Weight lag j

02:08.972 --> 02:13.053
by one minus j over m plus one: near echoes count almost

02:13.153 --> 02:17.757
fully, distant ones fade to nothing. It is the same triangle as

02:17.857 --> 02:22.386
in the covariance grid, so the estimate can never be negative.

02:23.466 --> 02:27.737
Back to inflation. Ignoring the echoes, the standard error of

02:27.837 --> 02:31.177
the mean is point one four. Counting them, it is

02:31.277 --> 02:34.760
about point two zero, and it keeps growing with m,

02:34.860 --> 02:39.275
because inflation remembers for years. The interval must widen.

02:40.366 --> 02:44.799
So: correlated observations carry fewer independent facts. The central

02:44.899 --> 02:48.036
limit theorem still holds, but its variance is the

02:48.136 --> 02:50.950
long-run variance, the sum of all the echoes.
